00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:02.000 position:63%
The Punic Wars between Rome and Carthage were&nbsp;
brutal affairs stretching out over a century and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:07.600 position:63%
costing hundreds of thousands of lives. By the&nbsp;
conclusion of the Second War, it was apparent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:14.560 position:63%
that Rome had emerged the ultimate victor. Yet, so&nbsp;
long as the city of Carthage drew breath, it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:22.160 position:63%
haunt Rome's psyche and stand as a grand prize&nbsp;
for any who might prove capable of finishing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:31.520 position:63%
job once and for all. Finally, in 149 BC, the Wolf&nbsp;
of Rome spotted an opportunity to make her move.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:38.720 position:63%
Carthage had been battered in a failed attempt to&nbsp;
defend itself against Numidian aggressions and was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:45.760 position:63%
in no position to fight back against the might of&nbsp;
the legions. Thus they bowed to every demand the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:53.120 position:63%
envoys made. They burned their fleet, released&nbsp;
their war elephants, relinquished their arms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.120 --> 00:04:01.600 position:63%
and gave up their children as hostages. Yet at&nbsp;
each turn, Rome asked for more, culminating in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:08.000 position:63%
the impossible demand that they abandon their&nbsp;
city entirely. With nothing else to lose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:17.840 position:63%
the Carthaginians bolted the doors. They would go&nbsp;
down fighting. In spite of the overwhelming odds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:23.840 position:63%
the Lions of Africa put up an incredible&nbsp;
defense. Rather than the short conquest the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:31.120 position:63%
Romans expected, the siege dragged on for three&nbsp;
long years. No inch of ground was yielded without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:38.960 position:63%
a fight. No assault went unanswered without a&nbsp;
counterattack. No act of daring was unmatched by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:45.920 position:63%
staunch resolve. On multiple occasions, the tides&nbsp;
were very nearly turned against the invaders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:52.640 position:63%
Only with the masterful leadership of Scipio&nbsp;
Amelanis were the Romans finally able to break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:59.120 position:63%
the backs of the defenders. Here the console&nbsp;
led a brutal assault into the very heart of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:06.560 position:63%
the city. Fighting descended into brutal urban&nbsp;
combat from building to building as the legions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:13.600 position:63%
ground their way up to the last stronghold at&nbsp;
top the Bersa hill. Finally, with all hope lost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:22.560 position:63%
the Carthaginians surrendered to save the lives of&nbsp;
its remaining citizens. In this climactic moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:28.960 position:63%
as the city of Carthage burned around him,&nbsp;
Scipio Amelanis mused on the inevitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:34.400 position:63%
fate that would one day befall his&nbsp;
own city. With these final thoughts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:40.720 position:63%
he put a neat bow on the narrative of the&nbsp;
siege. However, for the people on the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:48.240 position:63%
the story of what happened in the&nbsp;
aftermath is its own fascinating saga.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:54.960 position:63%
The next phase of city conquest typically&nbsp;
involved the mass looting of its valuables.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.960 --> 00:06:01.520 position:63%
This could go on for many days as troops were&nbsp;
let loose to sate their greed and blood rage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:09.920 position:63%
However, Rome had learned that such unconstrained&nbsp;
looting was problematic for discipline. Indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:15.520 position:63%
the final assault upon Carthage had nearly&nbsp;
ground to a halt as troops disobeyed orders to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:23.200 position:63%
pillage local temples. In such a wild free-for-all&nbsp;
state, the army was susceptible to counterattack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:30.240 position:63%
and operational chaos. Thus, we hear that&nbsp;
Scipio wisely gave the soldiers a prescribed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:39.920 position:63%
number of days for plunder with the coordinated&nbsp;
collection of gold, silver, and temple gifts. Yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:45.360 position:63%
even with such precautions in place, we can&nbsp;
only imagine the ecstatic glee with which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:51.760 position:63%
the tens of thousands of soldiers now rushed&nbsp;
down the city streets to make their fortunes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:58.560 position:63%
A prime target would have been the holy sites&nbsp;
which often served as warehouses for treasuries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:04.000 position:63%
and offerings to the gods. As one of the&nbsp;
wealthiest cities of its age, there must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:11.040 position:63%
have been mounds of treasure to be hauled out&nbsp;
of them. Appion specifically mentions that these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:17.280 position:63%
included many prizes of war that had been taken&nbsp;
from the Sicilian Greeks over the centuries. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:23.840 position:63%
a diplomatic move of magnanimity, Scipio ordered&nbsp;
that they were to be set aside and returned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:29.520 position:63%
to their rightful owners. Other treasures to&nbsp;
emerge from the temples would have been the many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:37.520 position:63%
texts that had been inscribed onto gold plates.&nbsp;
These recorded the important deeds of the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:43.920 position:63%
likely including the great journeys of Hano and&nbsp;
Hamlo, who had ventured far into the Atlantic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:50.640 position:63%
Perhaps there even remained relics of their&nbsp;
voyage, like the preserved pelts of the hairy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:58.400 position:63%
gorilli peoples they discovered. Unfortunately, we&nbsp;
must imagine that the etchings in foreign tongue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:03.840 position:63%
would not have been of much value to the troops&nbsp;
who merely saw a nice chunk of precious metal to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:10.720 position:63%
be traded or melted down. Besides the temples,&nbsp;
another magnet for lutters would have been the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:17.760 position:63%
estates of the wealthy. Here one would find many&nbsp;
personal belongings of value such as jewelry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:26.000 position:63%
perfumes, silverware, vasees, furniture,&nbsp;
rugs, clothes, statues, art pieces, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:34.240 position:63%
more. Other oddities might make for souvenirs and&nbsp;
keepsakes. The home of a far traveling merchant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:41.920 position:63%
for instance, might yield exotic trinkets&nbsp;
of ivory, pearl, tortoise shelled, silk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:49.440 position:63%
tyran cloth, and leopard fur. A spice trader&nbsp;
might have stores of saffron, cinnamon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:58.480 position:63%
and frankincense. A book collector might have toss&nbsp;
on agriculture, medicine, and navigation. The men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:04.240 position:63%
who stumbled into the home of Hannibal Barker,&nbsp;
meanwhile, would have taken just about anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:13.680 position:63%
that could be pried from its walls and floors as&nbsp;
trophies to their conquest of this legendary foe.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:21.840 position:63%
As mobs overwhelmed these more obvious targets,&nbsp;
other soldiers had to treasure hunt. Some tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:27.840 position:63%
their luck pilfering the workshops, stores, and&nbsp;
lower classes houses which filled out the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:34.800 position:63%
the city. Some set off into the outskirts of&nbsp;
the Magara district to try their luck in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:42.000 position:63%
sprawling gardens and estates. Yet others turned&nbsp;
to the corpses of the dead or the earth itself as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:51.280 position:63%
they sought out hidden caches buried away to elude&nbsp;
them. All of these items, the collective wealth of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:57.920 position:63%
an entire civilization, would have been piled up&nbsp;
in open spaces of Carthage before being hauled off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:06.160 position:63%
to be loaded onto Roman ships. From this horde,&nbsp;
select items were chosen to be used as prizes for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:14.800 position:63%
the soldiers. These Scipio handed out to the men&nbsp;
as prizes for valorous deeds. Such ceremonies were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:21.600 position:63%
likely accompanied by important rituals. Aion,&nbsp;
for instance, reports that piles of arms and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:31.200 position:63%
engines of war were set ablaze as an offering&nbsp;
to Mars and Manurva for this great victory.

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:37.280 position:63%
While all this was taking place, fast&nbsp;
ships raced back to Rome with the news.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:43.840 position:63%
Official messengers would have been sent to&nbsp;
formally declare the results to the Senate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:49.280 position:63%
However, such momentous tidings would have been&nbsp;
difficult to keep secret and rumors quickly spread&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:55.600 position:63%
across the city. I'll now read from Aion at length&nbsp;
as he gives a great accounting of the reaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.600 --> 00:11:03.680 position:63%
among the people. Quote, they were so excited over&nbsp;
this victory that they could hardly believe it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:09.600 position:63%
And they asked each other over and over again&nbsp;
whether it was really true that Carthage was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:15.520 position:63%
destroyed. And so they gabbled the whole night&nbsp;
telling how the arms of the Carthaginians were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:22.320 position:63%
got away from them and how contrary to expectation&nbsp;
they supplied themselves with others. How they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:28.560 position:63%
lost their ships and built a great fleet out of&nbsp;
old material. how the mouth of the harbor was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:35.520 position:63%
closed, yet they managed to open another in a few&nbsp;
days. They talked about the height of the walls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:42.960 position:63%
and the size of the stones and the fires that so&nbsp;
often destroyed the engines. They pictured to each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:49.440 position:63%
other the whole war as though it was just taking&nbsp;
place under their own eyes, suiting the action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:57.280 position:63%
to the word. And they seemed to see Scipio&nbsp;
on the ladders, on shipboard, at the gates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.280 --> 00:12:09.280 position:63%
in the battles, and darting hither and thither.&nbsp;
In this way, the people of Rome passed the night.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:15.520 position:63%
The next day there were sacrifices and&nbsp;
solemn processions to the gods by tribes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:23.920 position:63%
also games and spectacles of various kinds. The&nbsp;
Senate sent 10 of the noblest of their own number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:34.160 position:63%
as deputies to arrange the affairs of Africa in&nbsp;
conjunction with Scipio to the advantage of Rome.

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:39.600 position:63%
It would have taken this delegation a&nbsp;
few days to reach the shores of Carthage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:46.400 position:63%
Upon arrival, they must have been greeted&nbsp;
to an incredible sight of Roman troops in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:54.560 position:63%
the throws of delirious victory, partying in&nbsp;
their camp amidst the spoils of war. Likely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.560 --> 00:13:00.320 position:63%
the envoys would have wished to take a tour of&nbsp;
the battlefield for themselves. A natural starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:06.080 position:63%
point would have been the great observation tower,&nbsp;
which had been built to supervise the siege.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:11.600 position:63%
From here they could marvel at the Roman&nbsp;
entrenchments, the grand mole that had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:19.200 position:63%
been raised across the harbor and the vast city&nbsp;
that lay beyond. Accompanying these sites would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:25.360 position:63%
have been a narration by Scipio, who'd likely&nbsp;
gave them a blowby-blow of the events before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:32.560 position:63%
then leading them on a walking tour through the&nbsp;
scar that had been carved through the defenses.

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:39.840 position:63%
But as the daylight hours slipped by, they&nbsp;
still must have had many questions to ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:48.320 position:63%
of Rome's conquering hero. Scipio would have&nbsp;
been more than happy to regail them over a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:54.320 position:63%
feasting dinner. Though this goes unrecorded, we&nbsp;
can imagine how it might have featured a display&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.320 --> 00:14:00.880 position:63%
of the trophies, the foods, and perhaps&nbsp;
even the people of Carthage. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:06.480 position:63%
the defending general Hazdrabal had been&nbsp;
captured alive. Who knows what humiliations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:12.400 position:63%
he would now been subjected to. Knowing Roman&nbsp;
sensibilities, what could have been better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.400 --> 00:14:21.920 position:63%
than having this emasculated man serve the&nbsp;
meals at top the very plates of his homeland?

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:27.840 position:63%
Next on the agenda seems to have been to decide&nbsp;
what was to become of the city of Carthage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:36.080 position:63%
To this end, the Senate had apparently already&nbsp;
made up their minds. Appion states, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:39.520 position:63%
"They decreed that if anything&nbsp;
was still left of Carthage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.520 --> 00:14:45.760 position:63%
Scipio would obliterate it and that nobody&nbsp;
should be allowed to live there. Direful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:50.560 position:63%
threats were leveled against any who&nbsp;
should disobey and chiefly against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.560 --> 00:15:00.240 position:63%
rebuilding of Bersa or Maggara. But it&nbsp;
was not forbidden to go upon the ground.

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:07.200 position:63%
Thus, the manpower of the legions was now directed&nbsp;
towards the brickby brick deconstruction of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:12.240 position:63%
city. Their motivation was likely aided&nbsp;
by the idea that such work may yet turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:19.520 position:63%
up even more treasures that they had missed in&nbsp;
their scouring of Carthage. And so they toiled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.520 --> 00:15:27.840 position:63%
in the hot sun. Down came the walls. Down came&nbsp;
the harbor. Down came the temples, the schools,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:34.960 position:63%
the shops, the fountains, the colonates,&nbsp;
and the gardens. As these edififices of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.960 --> 00:15:44.160 position:63%
once proud empire came crumbling down, they&nbsp;
entombed its inhabitants and their history.

00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:51.600 position:63%
However, contrary to popular belief, Rome did not&nbsp;
entirely wipe Carthage from the face of the map.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:57.920 position:63%
The contents of its great library was spared with&nbsp;
preservation efforts specifically being earmarked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:08.560 position:63%
for Margo's famous agricultural encyclopedia.&nbsp;
Plenny the Elder reports as follows. quote

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:14.880 position:63%
after the taking of Carthage that when it gave&nbsp;
away the city's libraries to the petty kings of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:22.720 position:63%
Africa the Senate passed a resolution that&nbsp;
in his case alone Margos 28 volumes should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:29.440 position:63%
be translated into Latin in spite of the fact&nbsp;
that Marcus Kato had already compiled his book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.440 --> 00:16:37.920 position:63%
of precepts and that the task should be given to&nbsp;
persons acquainted with the Carthaginian language.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:48.160 position:63%
An accomplishment in which Desimus Sylvanas, a man&nbsp;
of most distinguished family, surpassed everybody.

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:54.480 position:63%
Thus, we see that even in their greatest&nbsp;
foe, Rome still found value in its works.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.480 --> 00:17:01.200 position:63%
Other admirers seem to have been King Juba II of&nbsp;
Moritania, who reportedly collected much of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:07.600 position:63%
had once been housed in the state library&nbsp;
of Carthage. Other Punic works apparently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.600 --> 00:17:14.720 position:63%
still remained in circulation, either in their&nbsp;
original form or in translations. St. Augustine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:21.760 position:63%
of the fifth century AD, for example, writes to&nbsp;
his followers, quote, "If the Punic language is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:28.320 position:63%
rejected by you, you virtually deny what has&nbsp;
been admitted by most learned men that many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.320 --> 00:17:35.840 position:63%
things have been wisely preserved from oblivion&nbsp;
in books written in the Punic tongue." We'll have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:41.600 position:63%
more on this cultural preservation shortly. For&nbsp;
now though, we should wrap up the destruction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.600 --> 00:17:51.200 position:63%
of Carthage with its most iconic moment, the&nbsp;
salting of the city. It's a dramatic scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.200 --> 00:17:56.960 position:63%
the spreading of salt to ensure that no life&nbsp;
could ever take root in this ancestral home of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.960 --> 00:18:05.520 position:63%
Rome's ultimate rival. Unfortunately, this tale&nbsp;
is not mentioned by any of our ancient sources.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:12.400 position:63%
Instead, it first made its appearance in the&nbsp;
mid-9th century. Even then, academics were quick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:21.760 position:63%
to rebuke the claim. However, clearly the power&nbsp;
of the imagery has allowed the idea to endure. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.760 --> 00:18:28.400 position:63%
where did it come from? Likely, some inspiration&nbsp;
was drawn from the sorting of other cities which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.400 --> 00:18:37.120 position:63%
were reported across the near east. The Bible, for&nbsp;
instance, speaks of what became of Shikim. Quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:43.360 position:63%
Aimilec fought against the city all that day. He&nbsp;
took the city and killed the people that were in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:51.440 position:63%
it, and he raised the city and sewed it with&nbsp;
salt. Other Bronze Age settlements reportedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:58.640 position:63%
faced similar destructions that were followed by&nbsp;
the sewing of salt, stone, or weeds. Yet even in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.640 --> 00:19:05.200 position:63%
these cases, it is unlikely that entire cities&nbsp;
were being covered in salt. This would have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:12.640 position:63%
a herculean undertaking involving masses amounts&nbsp;
of labor, let alone the wasting of thousands upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.640 --> 00:19:18.000 position:63%
thousands of tons of precious resources.&nbsp;
Instead, it is more appropriate to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:26.640 position:63%
of these acts as small symbolic rituals meant to&nbsp;
harm the site on a more deeply spiritual level.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:33.600 position:63%
Evidence for this sort of thing happening to&nbsp;
Carthage is actually present in a tiny excerpt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:42.160 position:63%
from the digest of Modestinus. In it, he passingly&nbsp;
remarks, quote, "If the right of use is assigned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.160 --> 00:19:50.960 position:63%
to a city and a plow is drawn over it, it ceases&nbsp;
to be a city, as for example, Carthage suffered."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.960 --> 00:19:57.600 position:63%
The context here is that ancient cities often&nbsp;
had a sacred boundary which was plowed as one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.600 --> 00:20:05.120 position:63%
of the first steps of establishing their legal&nbsp;
and religious existence. Rome's own pomeriium is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.120 --> 00:20:12.960 position:63%
quite famous and carried with it specific rules&nbsp;
such as a ban on any weapons inside of it. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.960 --> 00:20:17.280 position:63%
we don't know anything about the nature of&nbsp;
Carthage's boundary, clearly there is some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:22.960 position:63%
record that it was intentionally violated.&nbsp;
Perhaps it is this mention of a plowing of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.960 --> 00:20:31.760 position:63%
the city boundary that was later contorted&nbsp;
into the story of it being sewn with salt.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.760 --> 00:20:38.880 position:63%
But regardless of the nature of this final act,&nbsp;
the destruction of Carthage was now complete. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.880 --> 00:20:48.400 position:63%
what of its people? Carthage is estimated to have&nbsp;
had a peak population of around 300,000 people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.400 --> 00:20:53.680 position:63%
This number would have been greatly diminished by&nbsp;
the outbreak of the Third Punic War and certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.680 --> 00:20:59.680 position:63%
by the time the city was ultimately cut&nbsp;
off by siege. Of those trapped within,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.680 --> 00:21:06.880 position:63%
we have no indication of how many would die from&nbsp;
battle, disease, starvation, or slaughter by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.880 --> 00:21:13.760 position:63%
end of the 3-year struggle. Our only data for the&nbsp;
number of survivors comes from Aion, who claims&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.760 --> 00:21:21.600 position:63%
that 50,000 men, women, and children were taken&nbsp;
captive when the citadel finally surrendered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.600 --> 00:21:28.240 position:63%
Perhaps tens of thousands more would be counted&nbsp;
in the days that followed as Rome conducted its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.240 --> 00:21:35.200 position:63%
sweep of the city. Adding slightly to this number&nbsp;
would also be the 300 children who had been sent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.200 --> 00:21:41.840 position:63%
away as hostages at the start of the war. However,&nbsp;
the history books are silent of what have become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.840 --> 00:21:49.840 position:63%
of them by this point. Taken as a percentage of&nbsp;
the peak population, these figures together leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:49.840 --> 00:22:02.000 position:63%
Carthage with only a 17% survival rate. A brutal&nbsp;
statistic, even if it is only a rough estimate.

00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:07.920 position:63%
Whatever the final tally, these civilians were&nbsp;
all now prisoners of Rome. As such, they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:14.560 position:63%
have been chained as slaves to be paraded in&nbsp;
a triumph and ultimately sold off at auction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.560 --> 00:22:21.760 position:63%
across the empire. It is impossible to trace&nbsp;
what happened to these individuals. Presumably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.760 --> 00:22:28.880 position:63%
they would have found themselves put to work in&nbsp;
a variety of jobs. For many, this meant working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.880 --> 00:22:35.280 position:63%
manual labor in the fields, the streets, and the&nbsp;
mines. But Carthage was not some mere backwater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:35.280 --> 00:22:42.560 position:63%
barbarian village. Many of its citizens were well&nbsp;
educated and likely spoke Greek or Latin. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.560 --> 00:22:48.960 position:63%
we can expect that a good number of them served&nbsp;
as specialized slaves who, like the Greeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.960 --> 00:22:57.840 position:63%
might be used as teachers, scribes, accountants,&nbsp;
navigators, or other roles befitting their skills.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:03.920 position:63%
Perhaps such talents may have ultimately&nbsp;
allowed them to earn their freedom either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:09.280 position:63%
for themselves or their children.&nbsp;
Eventually, over the generations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.280 --> 00:23:16.000 position:63%
these scattered Carthaginians may have attempted&nbsp;
to form their own small communities abroad or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:22.800 position:63%
perhaps even attempted a return to Africa. But&nbsp;
whatever new homes they found, it must have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.800 --> 00:23:31.600 position:63%
impossible to avoid the long traumatic shadow&nbsp;
that was the destruction of their homeland.

00:23:31.600 --> 00:23:37.520 position:63%
Returning now to the immediate aftermath&nbsp;
of the fall of Carthage. These survivors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.520 --> 00:23:42.640 position:63%
would have joined the legions as they began&nbsp;
to demobilize for their return to Italy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.200 --> 00:23:48.800 position:63%
Along the way, the fleets may have paraded&nbsp;
itself along the shores of Sicily and Italy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:48.800 --> 00:23:55.280 position:63%
as had been done in the prior Punic Wars. Once&nbsp;
home, the troops would have marched up to Rome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.280 --> 00:24:03.440 position:63%
and encamped in the field of Mars. Here,&nbsp;
more rituals and ceremonies were to be had.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:11.440 position:63%
The main event, however, would have been&nbsp;
the upcoming triumph of Scipio Amelianas.

00:24:11.440 --> 00:24:16.240 position:63%
In the traditional manner, this involved&nbsp;
the general leading his troops in a parade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.240 --> 00:24:21.680 position:63%
route across the city. Here would be&nbsp;
displayed the captives of the war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:21.680 --> 00:24:28.320 position:63%
their spoils, the victorious soldiers, and&nbsp;
most importantly, the general riding in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:28.320 --> 00:24:37.520 position:63%
chariot while garbed in a triumphal toga. Their&nbsp;
face painted red in honor of the god Jupiter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:37.520 --> 00:24:44.240 position:63%
It was the biggest moment in a politician's&nbsp;
career. Scipio's triumph was sure to have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.240 --> 00:24:50.480 position:63%
one for the ages. Unfortunately, we have&nbsp;
no detailed accounting of the event. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:50.480 --> 00:24:57.120 position:63%
that remains is this tantalizing passage&nbsp;
from Aion. Quote, "When all was finished,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:57.120 --> 00:25:03.920 position:63%
he sailed for home and was awarded the most&nbsp;
glorious triumph that had ever been known,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:10.640 position:63%
splendid with gold and gorged with statues&nbsp;
and votive offerings that the Carthaginians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:10.640 --> 00:25:18.160 position:63%
had gathered from all parts of the world through&nbsp;
all time, the fruit of their countless victories."

00:25:19.840 --> 00:25:26.160 position:63%
Libby further drops a oneline hint that&nbsp;
Scipio's triumph was also accompanied by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:26.160 --> 00:25:33.120 position:63%
organized games in which some of the legionary&nbsp;
deserters and runaways were killed by wild&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:33.120 --> 00:25:40.080 position:63%
animals. Clearly the point being made here&nbsp;
was that no one should ever doubt the might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:40.080 --> 00:25:46.880 position:63%
of Rome. For Scipio in particular, he had now&nbsp;
rightfully earned the personal claim to bear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:46.880 --> 00:25:55.600 position:63%
the agnomomen Africanis for having made&nbsp;
this message clear to the Carthaginians.

00:25:55.600 --> 00:26:01.200 position:63%
As the fanfare finally subsided in Italy,&nbsp;
we might now wonder what became of the city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:09.200 position:63%
of Carthage. Here it was decreed that Aria&nbsp;
would govern its lands on an annual basis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:09.200 --> 00:26:16.000 position:63%
His first order of business was to sort out the&nbsp;
political fallout from the war. Accordingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:21.600 position:63%
Appion writes, quote, "The towns that&nbsp;
had allied themselves with the enemy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:21.600 --> 00:26:27.760 position:63%
it was decided to destroy to the last&nbsp;
one. To those who had aided the Romans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:27.760 --> 00:26:34.640 position:63%
there was an allotment of lands by the sword.&nbsp;
And first of all to the Uticans was given the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:34.640 --> 00:26:43.760 position:63%
territory of Carthage itself extending as far as&nbsp;
Hippo. Upon all the rest a tribute was imposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:43.760 --> 00:26:55.200 position:63%
both a land tax and a personal tax upon men and&nbsp;
women alike. Thus began Roman rule of Africa.

00:26:56.720 --> 00:27:04.160 position:63%
As this was going on, it seems that the city of&nbsp;
Carthage itself remained untouched. A carcass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:04.160 --> 00:27:13.280 position:63%
left to rot in the hot sun. Presumably, however,&nbsp;
it did have visitors, perhaps curious neighbors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:13.280 --> 00:27:20.160 position:63%
grieving relatives, greedy robbers, or distant&nbsp;
tourists approached to catch a glimpse of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:27.360 position:63%
aftermath of the siege. From a distance, not much&nbsp;
would be seen besides a mound of rubble. Those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:27.360 --> 00:27:35.760 position:63%
who ventured closer might begin to spot signs of&nbsp;
the struggle. A palisade here, an arrow there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:35.760 --> 00:27:44.880 position:63%
a torn leather shoe half submerged in mud. This&nbsp;
may have been enough for most, but for others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:44.880 --> 00:27:51.040 position:63%
circling carryan birds would have marked where&nbsp;
the true grizzly aftermath could be found.

00:27:53.280 --> 00:27:58.720 position:63%
For the people or the scavenging animals who&nbsp;
made their way inside the walls of Carthage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:58.720 --> 00:28:07.600 position:63%
they must have been met by a haunting landscape.&nbsp;
Ash strewn heaps of wood and stone whose chaotic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:07.600 --> 00:28:15.120 position:63%
forms were nonetheless neatly arranged in the&nbsp;
identifiable plan of the city. Amidst the ruins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:15.120 --> 00:28:20.160 position:63%
some buildings still proudly held their&nbsp;
form, while others teetered in the wind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:20.160 --> 00:28:32.160 position:63%
as disfigured amputees. In their shadows lay&nbsp;
the detritus of war and peace. Broken shields,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:32.160 --> 00:28:42.320 position:63%
shattered pots, tattered garments, a&nbsp;
soldier's helmet, a merchant's abacus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:55.440 position:63%
an officer's belt, a musician's flute,&nbsp;
a warhor's saddle, a child's toy.

00:28:55.440 --> 00:29:02.800 position:63%
All awaiting the owner, who&nbsp;
would never return. Here

00:29:02.800 --> 00:29:09.280 position:63%
and there were the bleached bones of lost souls&nbsp;
picked clean by the elements and the animals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.280 --> 00:29:16.640 position:63%
Beneath them lay the tens of thousands more&nbsp;
which had been buried by the ash, the debris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:16.640 --> 00:29:23.200 position:63%
all the dirt of mass graves. Those who&nbsp;
witnessed this aftermath of the doom of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:23.200 --> 00:29:31.520 position:63%
Carthage must have returned home to spread&nbsp;
tales of the horrors. Rumors and folk legend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:31.520 --> 00:29:38.240 position:63%
must have spread about what lay in the haunted&nbsp;
ruins. Shortly after this period, we hear from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:38.240 --> 00:29:49.520 position:63%
Libby that Africa was ravaged by a plague of&nbsp;
locusts. Perhaps the lands truly were cursed.

00:29:49.520 --> 00:29:57.280 position:63%
But at the end of the day, the strategic location&nbsp;
of Carthage was too important to abandon forever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:57.280 --> 00:30:04.720 position:63%
Indeed, within 14 years, Gas Graas would&nbsp;
push for legislation to found a colony on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:04.720 --> 00:30:12.400 position:63%
its ruins. According to Appion, the plans called&nbsp;
for an initial wave of 6,000 settlers. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:12.400 --> 00:30:20.320 position:63%
as the boundary stakes of the new city were being&nbsp;
erected, wolves emerged and obliterated them. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:20.320 --> 00:30:29.920 position:63%
the superstitious Romans, this was a grave omen.&nbsp;
The mission was immediately abandoned. Just a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:29.920 --> 00:30:36.880 position:63%
years later, the general Scipio Amelanis would&nbsp;
mysteriously be found dead in his bedroom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:36.880 --> 00:30:43.840 position:63%
Foul play was suspected on behalf of his&nbsp;
wife or his political opponents. Others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:43.840 --> 00:30:52.560 position:63%
we might imagine, feared that the ghosts&nbsp;
of Carthage had got their revenge.

00:30:52.560 --> 00:30:58.320 position:63%
It would not be for another 88 years that the&nbsp;
prospect of a Carthaginian colony was raised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:58.320 --> 00:31:05.680 position:63%
once more by Julius Caesar. The decision&nbsp;
had apparently come to him in a dream.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:05.680 --> 00:31:13.200 position:63%
Yet when he was murdered in 44 BC, the Republic&nbsp;
was once more cast into civil war and the plans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:22.320 position:63%
scrapped again. Eventually, his heir, Augustus,&nbsp;
would take up the mission. He sent 3,000 Roman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:22.320 --> 00:31:31.760 position:63%
settlers to found a new city known as the&nbsp;
colony Ulia Concordio Cathago. Aion notes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:31.760 --> 00:31:37.920 position:63%
quote, "It was located not on the site of&nbsp;
the old one, but very near it in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:37.920 --> 00:31:44.080 position:63%
avoid the ancient curse. Thus, the Romans&nbsp;
took Africa away from the Carthaginians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:44.080 --> 00:31:51.760 position:63%
destroyed Carthage, and repopled it&nbsp;
again 102 years after its destruction.

00:31:53.120 --> 00:31:59.200 position:63%
In time, this new Carthage would rise&nbsp;
as a phoenix from the ashes to once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:59.200 --> 00:32:06.400 position:63%
more become the most prominent city in Roman&nbsp;
Africa. As it did so, the young settlement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:06.400 --> 00:32:13.280 position:63%
would have been heavily influenced by its past&nbsp;
incarnation. Though Carthage had been destroyed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:13.280 --> 00:32:19.760 position:63%
the Punic language was still the primary&nbsp;
dialect of the region. Its cultural, religious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:19.760 --> 00:32:25.360 position:63%
and governing traditions had also been preserved&nbsp;
by the neighboring Phoenician cities and would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:25.360 --> 00:32:31.920 position:63%
now help steer its development. Even major&nbsp;
structures like the great Kthon harour would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:31.920 --> 00:32:40.400 position:63%
be resurrected in this new dawn of Carthage.&nbsp;
Its story shall have to wait for another time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:41.280 --> 00:32:47.840 position:63%
We hope you've appreciated this closer look at the&nbsp;
overlooked legacy of Carthage after its fall. On&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:47.840 --> 00:32:53.600 position:63%
the channel, we've done our best to bring this&nbsp;
fascinating civilization to life. If you'd like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:53.600 --> 00:32:59.280 position:63%
to learn more, definitely check out our series on&nbsp;
the social and military history of Carthage. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:59.280 --> 00:33:06.000 position:63%
can also grab an awesome poster of the city in&nbsp;
our store alongside a ton of other cool merch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:06.000 --> 00:33:11.520 position:63%
A big thanks to our fans on Patreon and YouTube&nbsp;
memberships for supporting the channel and to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:11.520 --> 00:33:16.320 position:63%
researchers, writers, and artists for making&nbsp;
this episode possible. We couldn't have done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:16.320 --> 00:33:21.440 position:63%
it without this team and this community. If&nbsp;
you enjoyed this episode, be sure to like and&nbsp;&nbsp;

